# UI

Follow `docs/04-design-spec.md` for layout, states, interactions, motion and accessibility.

## React

- Function components with typed props; one component per file, named in PascalCase like the file (`ResultCard.tsx`).
- **Modular components:** small, with one job each (e.g. `SearchBar`, `ResultTabs`, `ResultCard`, `DetailOverlay`). A component that grows a second job gets split.
- Components only display: search logic stays in `src/search/`, and components call it, never reimplement it.
- Follow the standard React conventions: hooks only at the top level, stable `key`s from the data (never the array index), controlled inputs, derived values computed instead of stored in state, effects only to sync with something outside React.
- Each component sits next to its stylesheet in its own folder, inside its feature: `features/results/ResultCard/ResultCard.tsx` + `ResultCard.scss`. Generic pieces go in `shared/`, the shell in `app/`.

## Styling

- **No inline styles or embedded styling** (`style={{…}}`, CSS-in-JS): all styling lives in `.scss` files.
- **One class per element.** Class names say what the element *is*, in BEM form where it helps: `result-card`, `result-card__name`, `result-card__match`.
- **Variants without a second class:** prefer state attributes, which also serve accessibility (`[aria-selected="true"]`, `[aria-pressed="true"]`, `[data-exact="true"]`). If a real variant is needed, write it as its own full class (`result-card--compact`) that includes the shared styles through a mixin.
- **Nest BEM under its block:** a block's elements, modifiers and states are written inside the block's rule with `&` (`&__name`, `&--compact`, `&[aria-selected='true']`), so each block is one rule tree. One block per stylesheet; nest an element's own states and media queries inside it, but never an element inside another element.

  ```scss
  .result-card {
    padding: $space-3;

    &[data-exact='true'] { … }

    &__name {
      font-family: $font-display;

      @include desktop { … }
    }

    &__match { … }
  }
  ```
  
- **`@include`s first:** mixins without a block come before the declarations, then a blank line (`@include desktop { … }` stays after them). Checked by `npm run lint:styles`, fixed by `npx stylelint "src/**/*.scss" --fix`.
- **No tag selectors**, except for app-wide base styles (fonts and sizes for `body`, `h1`–`h3`, a reset). Everything else is selected by its class.
- **Mixins whenever a style pattern repeats** (pixel borders, the exact-match glow, card layout, focus ring, breakpoints, screen-reader-only text), defined once in a shared partial.
- **Text styles come from `styles/_typography.scss`:** `@use 'styles/typography' as Typography;` then `@include Typography.small-display;` (or `title-large-display`, `title-display`, `label-display`, `tiny-display`, `body`, `input-body`). Never set `font-family`, `font-size` or `line-height` by hand in a component.
- **Variables for every design value:** colours (palette roles and type colours), spacing in `rem`, font sizes, breakpoints, durations. No magic numbers repeated across files.
- Mobile first: base styles for phones, one breakpoint for desktop.
- **Comments in stylesheets say why, never what.** No comment that describes what a rule does or where a mixin is used; only a reason, a constraint or a number the code can't explain (`// 4.8:1 on $surface`, `// VT323 renders small: body text never below 1.125rem`). When in doubt, leave it out.

## Accessibility

- **Every image has an `alt`:** the Pokémon's name for its picture; `alt=""` for purely decorative images.
- **Every control has an accessible name:** visible text, or an `aria-label` for icon buttons ("Add Pikachu to the Pokédex", "Select Pikachu", "Close").
- Everything works with the keyboard, focus is always visible, and overlays move focus in and back out.
- No information carried by colour alone: pair it with text or a symbol (✓, ✕, ▲ ▼ =).
- Respect `prefers-reduced-motion`.

## Results

- **Show what matched** on every result (the speed for "fast electric", the move and its effect for "sleep"), not why the engine chose it.
- Exact matches (score 1) are highlighted; close matches follow in the same list.
- Pokémon images are links to GitHub: always show a placeholder when an image fails to load, and keep the name and types visible.
- Hide controls for features that aren't built.
